1- INCLUDE GAURDS
2- #include<stddef.h> in files that use size_t
3- Memory management Check the return values of malloc() and realloc()
4- Use a temporary pointer with realloc() to avoid losing the original allocation if reallocation fails
5- Review the list-freeing functions, as some implementations leak memory or do not free any nodes. 
6- Linked-list implementation DoublyNode_Create() is missing a return statement.
7- Several insertion and deletion functions do not safely handle empty lists or lists containing only one node.
8- The circular-list free functions have loop conditions that prevent them from executing correctly.
9- Avoid declaring node pointers as const in functions that modify or free the nodes
10- Validate indexes before allocating new nodes to prevent memory leaks
11- Error handling Returning -1 from stack and queue operations is ambiguous because -1 may be a valid stored value
12- Consider returning a status value such as bool and using an output parameter for the retrieved value.
13- Invalid-index operations should not return NULL if doing so may cause the caller to lose the original list head
14- Dynamic array Reset the length and capacity when freeing the array
15- Use size_t consistently for sizes, indexes, and loop counters
16- Queue design The current dequeue operation removes the first array element and shifts all remaining elements, resulting in O(n) complexity
17- A circular-buffer implementation would provide O(1) enqueue and dequeue operations
18- VOID* OR TYPED MACROS WRAPPERS FOR GENERICS